00:01:27.092 --> 00:01:31.500
If we were to watch this video together&nbsp;
with our usual microcosmos mindset,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.769
our focus would be on that bristleworm&nbsp;
inching its way through the background.

00:01:36.769 --> 00:01:39.050
We would watch it poke its head around as its

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.480
body sharpens and blurs under&nbsp;
the lens of our microscope.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.966
And those bright shining objects&nbsp;
it fumbles its way through would

00:01:46.966 --> 00:01:52.896
simply be props for our wormy star,&nbsp;
providing a mythical quality to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.896 --> 00:01:58.117
surroundings that make it even harder&nbsp;
to tear our gaze away from the screen.

00:01:58.408 --> 00:02:04.394
We might treat those grains the same way we&nbsp;
treat the bits of leaves or moss or branches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.394 --> 00:02:09.300
that decorate our other slides, which is to&nbsp;
say that we might note them and then move on.

00:02:09.776 --> 00:02:10.824
But not today.

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:17.227
Today, we’re going to shift our focus and&nbsp;
let those grains be the focus of our show.

00:02:17.227 --> 00:02:21.063
More specifically, we’re going to talk about sand.

00:02:26.001 --> 00:02:30.720
James, our master of microscopes, gets&nbsp;
samples of sand from beaches all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.840
the world to help in his quest to learn more&nbsp;
about interstitial ciliates—the single-celled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:43.538
organisms that live in the watery pockets that&nbsp;
exist between grains of sand on the beach.&nbsp;

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:48.780
In a previous episode, we have explored the ways&nbsp;
these organisms have adapted to the chaotic maze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:55.260
of sharp sand grains that is always shifting&nbsp;
and crashing around them with the waves. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.600
for this episode, we’re going to experience that&nbsp;
sand in a slightly more chill way, so that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.889
appreciate just how beautiful it is on its own.

00:03:06.677 --> 00:03:09.120
Now most of the grains you see in our sand samples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.940
are quartz, gleaming with this rainbow sheen as&nbsp;
polarized light from our microscope hits them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:19.426
bouncing off the silicon and oxygen&nbsp;
atoms arranged within the mineral.&nbsp;

00:03:20.018 --> 00:03:26.640
The process of making these sand grains at the&nbsp;
beach is like an endless voyage. It begins with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.100
the wearing down of rocks, as physical and&nbsp;
chemical forces slowly chip away at them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:39.681
and send pieces sailing down rivers and streams.&nbsp;
And eventually those pieces will reach the ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.681 --> 00:03:44.280
where the waves and tides around&nbsp;
them erode the pieces even smaller.&nbsp;

00:03:44.925 --> 00:03:51.360
Not all of the minerals that journey to the ocean&nbsp;
will last as long as these grains do. Some of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:57.540
are more unstable, making it easier for them to&nbsp;
break down. But quartz and other minerals like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:04.680
feldspar are more durable, and that keeps them&nbsp;
around for much, much longer. But over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:10.930
the ocean will wear those bits of sand&nbsp;
down into finer and finer grains.&nbsp;

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:16.020
This ceaseless process has been going on&nbsp;
in some beaches for thousands of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:22.609
if not longer. And the result is perhaps a&nbsp;
reflection of what beaches can be in general:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.609 --> 00:04:27.780
a place that brings things—or people—from&nbsp;
far away, and yet they reflect the local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:34.792
environment as well. The minerals and tourists may&nbsp;
be distant, but the essence is far more specific.&nbsp;

00:04:34.792 --> 00:04:40.867
After all, beaches are not just sand and&nbsp;
water. They are also an ecosystem full of life.

00:04:41.668 --> 00:04:46.020
Which means, yes, that they must&nbsp;
also be full of death.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:54.000
There are of course the fragmented&nbsp;
shells that have washed to shore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.230
hinting at creatures large and small whose&nbsp;
bodies relied upon them for protection.&nbsp;

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:05.220
And then there are structures like that single&nbsp;
point lancing through the middle of this slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:11.700
like a transparent needle. That was once part&nbsp;
of the exoskeleton of a sponge, a part called a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:17.520
spicule that acted like a brick glued together&nbsp;
by a collagen-like material called spongin.&nbsp;

00:05:18.822 --> 00:05:24.180
There’s another spicule here, on the right side of&nbsp;
your screen, with three transparent ends radiating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:30.420
out from the center. These structures are unique&nbsp;
to each species of sponge, their shape and size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:37.080
and chemical make-up a marker of their identity.
That sense of individuality makes spicules kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:43.140
like sand. The minerals that accumulate into&nbsp;
sand grains reflect the geology of whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:48.420
has shaped the beach to begin with, and you don’t&nbsp;
always need a microscope to see those influences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:54.180
Perhaps most starkly, you can think of the&nbsp;
black sand beaches of Hawaii, made from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:00.300
erosion of volcanic material like basalt rock.
But even the more mundane brown sand you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.400
typically associate with beaches tells a&nbsp;
story. The brown color is the result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.980
minerals like feldspar or iron oxide, which&nbsp;
can render quartz into that brownish color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:17.393
But in beaches where quartz begins to exceed&nbsp;
those other minerals, the result is white sand.&nbsp;

00:06:17.911 --> 00:06:22.800
Though not all white sand is quartz. In&nbsp;
fact, not all beaches are simply a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:30.240
of rocks. Sometimes, they are literal tons of&nbsp;
poop. Really. In Hawaii, parrotfish will eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.580
algae that grow on coral reefs, which means the&nbsp;
parrotfish might also ingest some of the coral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:40.800
itself. And after digestion, the parrotfish&nbsp;
poop out calcium carbonate from the coral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:47.160
which makes its way to the beach as white sand.
And these are not the only beaches whose beauty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:53.820
is shaped by the life that lives in nearby waters.&nbsp;
In Bermuda, the famous pink of their beaches is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.280
the result of tiny unicellular organisms called&nbsp;
foraminifera, which live in calcium carbonate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:05.700
shells like the swirls you see here. Within those&nbsp;
beaches, those shells eventually get broken down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:11.629
and washed to shore, and reddish pigments&nbsp;
within them color their new surroundings.&nbsp;

00:07:11.629 --> 00:07:16.080
These foraminifera don’t seem to have&nbsp;
had the same pinkish effect on the sand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:21.360
where their shells ended up. The conditions&nbsp;
around them created a different landscape, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:28.980
distinct and multifaceted identity told through a&nbsp;
kaleidoscope of countless grains. A reminder that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:35.640
on this planet, wherever you look, the biology&nbsp;
is geological, and the geology is biological.&nbsp;

